Wikipedia: List of Linux distributions.
}: aimed at improving and simplifying Debian design. You could say that the idea is very similar to Ubuntu, but without "straying" so much from the stability and security that Debian offers.
It gained fame because, a while ago they sent you a free CD to your house with the system for you to try. It also became very popular because its philosophy was based on making a "Linux for human beings", trying to bring Linux closer to the common desktop user and not to the "geeks" programmers.
}: It is the Ubuntu variant but with the KDE desktop. This desktop looks more like Win 7, so if you like it, you will like Kubuntu.
}: it is one of the "completely free" distros, according to the FSF.
}: This is the commercial version based on Fedora. While new versions of Fedora come out every 6 months or so, RHEL ones usually come out every 18 to 24 months.
It was designed with two goals in mind: ease of use and stability. It is the favorite of many "geeks", although today it is not very popular.
}: This is a distro that is gaining popularity. It is based on slackware, which makes it safe and stable, and incorporates several very interesting proprietary tools.
}: In 2010, a group of former Mandriva employees, with the support of community members, announced that they had created a fork of Mandriva Linux. A new community-led distribution called Mageia was created.
}: This is a mini-distribution of Linux based on PCLinuxOS, which is oriented towards older hardware.
}: It is only 50 MB in size, yet provides a fully functional system. Absolutely recommended for old compus.
}: is aimed at users with some experience in these operating systems.
