The development team of LineageOS announced a few days ago the availability of the new version 19 of its operating system, in which several improvements have been made such as a built-in privacy-oriented firewall, compatibility with the Android build targets for cars, as well as documentation, among other things. For those who are new to LineageOS, you should know that this is the successor of CyanogenMod and an open source operating system for smartphones and tablets.
However, the LineageOS team clarifies that it will not provide official builds for these purposes, due to the fact that the user experience varies entirely depending on how well the device manufacturer has met the Trebles requirements. Please note that Android 12 diverged the goals of GSI and Emulator.
The volume panel introduced in Android 12 has been completely redone and replaced with a side-opening expansion panel.
The Updater app has seen a lot of bug fixes and improvements.
Various changes and improvements have been made to the FOSS Etar Calendar application which was integrated some time ago.
The extraction utilities now support extraction of most types of factory images or packaged OTA images, greatly simplifying device loading and block extraction.
Of the improvements that version 19 of LineageOS brings stands out for example the integrated privacy-oriented firewall, restricted network mode and data isolation features per application have been rewritten to take into account the new restricted network mode and AOSP GMP. In addition, the data restriction and network isolation features have been merged into a single implementation.
The Jelly web browser has seen a lot of bug fixes and improvements.
A number of bottom-up changes and improvements have been made to the Seedvault backup application.
the adb_root service is no longer bound to the build type property.
High polling rate support has been added to the SDK, allowing it to be enabled on supported devices.
