The Southwestern United States as we know it today would not have existed without the Colorado River. The river spans 1,450 miles across the area, irrigates farmland, provides hydroelectric power generation, provides drinking water for millions of people, and is a source of recreation and beauty in recreation areas and parks approved by the federal government along the way.
In short, if government agencies want to maintain the Southwest economy, they must act quickly. The latest research from the University of Arizona and Colorado State University shows that rising temperatures have reduced the amount of water in the Colorado River.
Co-author Bradly Udall told Science Daily: “The future of the Colorado River is far less optimistic than other recent assessments portrayed. The clear message for water managers is that they need to plan to significantly reduce river flow.
However, according to the latest research by researchers in Colorado and Arizona, drought accounts for only two-thirds of the river decline. They said that the remaining third of the loss was actually caused by climate change.
