A new survey commissioned by CareerBuilder found that most women over the age of 25 are delaying starting a family in order to focus on their careers.
Want to make money and save enough money to support the family is the number one reason for postponing family planning for men and women. However, although men and women may be equally content to focus on their own occupations, the two genders have little similarity in the field of professional expectations.
Rosemary Haefner, Chief Human Resources Officer of CareerBuilder, said: “Today’s workforce is growing, and men and women are waiting for their children until they reach their professional and financial goals.”
The gender difference in career expectations is obvious, because 44% of men expect to achieve a six-figure salary, while only 20% of women. More than one-third of women (34%) believe that their organization’s pay is unequal.
The number of men expected to become company owners has more than doubled (9% compared to 4% for women). The desire to become a vice president also reflects similar differences, with 5% of men expected to reach the level of vice president, while only 2% of women.
