Before we realize this reality, I must first ask, I wonder if there is a shortage of seabirds?
The claim that recycling plastic can prevent the death of seabirds is wrong. It turns out that recycling is the source of the problem, not the solution.
The recyclables escape from the roadside into the environment, and then the imperfect process of transportation, handling and storage. All of these occur outdoors, because this will greatly reduce Cost, the effect of indoor processing and storage of plastic waste is not good.
Since there is no cost to recycle most materials (especially plastics), subsidies make every step of the country’s “green” recycling system work. Once this “green” subsidy is paid, imagine this strange recycling tycoon choosing to avoid the cost of actually recycling all the plastic he receives.
Of course, if a recycler dumps tons of plastic into the ocean from time to time, he will sell less recycled plastic. However, the subsidy is transferred to the plastic INTO recycling facility, and the remaining costs are left to the open market.
As long as the public sees that government officials support the recycling industry, most of us are still happy and ignorant. They believe that someone will shred millions of plastics, melt them, and make new plastic products.
